<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Luxembourg</h1><p class="st-meta">963 CE – 1795 CE · capital Luxembourg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The castle-county on the Alzette that gave the Holy Roman Empire three emperors and grew into the duchy behind today's Grand Duchy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 963 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegfried-count-of-luxembourg">Siegfried</a> acquired the rock of Lucilinburhuc from the abbey of St Maximin at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and built a castle there. His descendants, and from 1136 the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-namur">Namur</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-limburg">Limburg</a> lines, expanded the county through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> was a bilingual land of German and French speakers. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry VII</a> became emperor in 1308, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">John the Blind</a>, king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a>, died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a> in 1346. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> raised the county to a duchy in 1354. The fortress city became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe's</a> strongest, later rebuilt by Vauban.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-of-gorlitz">Elisabeth of Görlitz</a> ceded the duchy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a> in 1443. It then passed with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-netherlands">Spanish Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/austrian-netherlands">Austrian Netherlands</a>, was briefly French in 1684–1697, and was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1795. It was revived as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg</a> in 1815.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="County of Luxembourg" width="320" height="410"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_County_of_Luxembourg_(1350).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>963 CE – 1795 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Luxembourg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Siegfried of Luxembourg, Henry VII, John the Blind</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luxembourgish and French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Luxembourg, Belgium</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Luxembourg, Luxembourg</dd></dl></aside></div>
